WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.900
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:03.900 --> 00:00:12.600
Today we are going to find out what happens when&nbsp;
several AI agents play a video game together. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:19.620
in particular, what happens if we put&nbsp;
25 of these amazing ChatGPT AI agents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.620 --> 00:00:28.680
give them motivations and memory, and put them&nbsp;
in a simulated town? The answer is magic. Magic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.680 --> 00:00:35.280
happens. What I mean by that is that interesting&nbsp;
social behaviors emerge. They plan their days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.280 --> 00:00:42.060
form relationships, invite each other to&nbsp;
parties, they love to talk and talk and talk,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:49.620
I wonder what these discussions are about?&nbsp;
Yes, of course, I am going to tell you soon.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:56.040
Initially, we find them asleep, and then they&nbsp;
wake up and start their morning routine. They&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.040 --> 00:01:01.740
brush their teeth, get in the shower, and&nbsp;
dress up. Or, like good Fellow Scholars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:09.540
they want to read papers, or get this, to work on&nbsp;
their own papers. Yes, really! That’s how we know&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.540 --> 00:01:17.340
that this is a good, Scholarly game. But all this&nbsp;
is not surprising. The surprising part starts now.

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:25.500
Remember, they are ChatGPT agents, so yes,&nbsp;
they converse in full natural language. Here&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:32.880
is an example conversation. John asks Tom&nbsp;
”Hey, have you heard anything new about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:39.540
upcoming mayoral election?” Then Tom answers,&nbsp;
“No, not really. Do you know who is running?”

00:01:39.540 --> 00:01:46.740
Yes, there really is an election going on in&nbsp;
this little simulated town. How cool is that?

00:01:46.740 --> 00:01:52.200
For instance, later Isabella says to&nbsp;
Tom: “I’m still weighing my options,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:58.320
but I’ve been discussing the election with Sam&nbsp;
Moore. What are your thoughts on him?” Where Tom,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:05.040
a separate ChatGPT agent says “To be honest,&nbsp;
I don’t like Sam Moore. I think he’s out of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:09.120
touch with the community and doesn’t&nbsp;
have our best interests at heart.”

00:02:09.120 --> 00:02:16.020
Wow. They ask each other’s opinions, and try&nbsp;
to be informed citizens for the elections.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:22.740
Really cool. So what is the result? The&nbsp;
result is that the life these agents live&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.740 --> 00:02:29.880
resemble the interactions of real humans.&nbsp;
Okay, but how much do they resemble real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.880 --> 00:02:35.100
humans? That will be a huge surprise, I&nbsp;
will tell you by the end of this video.

00:02:35.100 --> 00:02:42.120
What I also loved is that these agents also&nbsp;
engage in self-reflection. They think, who am&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.120 --> 00:02:48.420
I and what is important for me? For instance,&nbsp;
you see here how one of the agents, Klaus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.420 --> 00:02:57.720
reflects on his observations, and finds out that&nbsp;
he is highly dedicated to doing research. A Fellow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.720 --> 00:03:03.780
Scholar at heart I see. Excellent! Like it is&nbsp;
true for us humans too, reflection is important.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:10.140
Sometimes we have to look back, reflect and&nbsp;
learn some new lessons based on our experiences.

00:03:10.140 --> 00:03:17.520
Thus, these agents can also be asked what they are&nbsp;
the most excited about, they check their memories,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:24.300
and Isabella finds out that she is most&nbsp;
excited about throwing a Valentine’s Day Party.

00:03:24.300 --> 00:03:31.440
And that is one of the most interesting parts of&nbsp;
the paper, because of two reasons. Question 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:39.540
is how did this party come about, and question 2,&nbsp;
did the party really happen? The party came about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:45.300
because one of the scientists specified to the&nbsp;
character that it wants to throw a Valentine’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.300 --> 00:03:54.300
Day party. The AI heard this as its own inner&nbsp;
voice, very cool, and tried to act on it. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:54.300 --> 00:04:02.340
here comes question 2. Does the party happen, and&nbsp;
if so, how? Organizing this is not easy at all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:09.300
not even in a small simulated game. You see, lots&nbsp;
of things have to happen for this to take place:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.300 --> 00:04:14.700
all of the characters have to know about&nbsp;
the party, and they also have to show up.

00:04:14.700 --> 00:04:21.120
But she really wants this party to happen, so&nbsp;
she tells our resident Fellow Scholar, Klaus,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:28.440
who then fortunately tells Abigail about&nbsp;
it, and look at that! Isabella hears back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:28.440 --> 00:04:34.440
about the party from someone she didn’t&nbsp;
even invite yet. Information is flowing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:40.980
in this little city like it would in a&nbsp;
real city among real humans. I love it.

00:04:40.980 --> 00:04:47.520
Quoting the paper, “the agents autonomously spread&nbsp;
invitations to the party over the next two days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.260
make new acquaintances, ask each&nbsp;
other out on dates to the party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.260 --> 00:05:00.540
and coordinate to show up for the party together&nbsp;
at the right time.” Wow. And the answer is yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.540 --> 00:05:04.440
the party indeed happened. Good job, little AIs!

00:05:04.440 --> 00:05:08.580
And there is one more thing that&nbsp;
I would love to show you about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.580 --> 00:05:15.480
this paper. So here is where I fell off&nbsp;
the chair. Look. What? Are you kidding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.480 --> 00:05:22.320
or is this real? Scientists at Google also asked&nbsp;
a bunch of humans to play the game, here they are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:30.840
and then they asked a separate set of evaluators&nbsp;
who found the AIs behavior more humanlike than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.840 --> 00:05:39.120
humans themselves. Yes, they are more human than&nbsp;
humans. According to evaluators. Wow. Of course,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.120 --> 00:05:44.340
every test like this has its limits, and&nbsp;
note that this is just a simple game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:49.620
with simple interactions, not real life.&nbsp;
But this still keeps me up at night.

00:05:49.620 --> 00:05:54.720
Some agents accepted the party&nbsp;
invitation, and some didn’t. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.720 --> 00:06:00.060
this decision came back to them reflecting&nbsp;
back on who they are, what their goals are,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:07.320
what their schedule is. This very much sounds like&nbsp;
human behavior. They have their own little lives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:14.520
and their own preferences. Little virtual friends.&nbsp;
Really cool experiment. What a time to be alive!

00:07:10.080 --> 00:07:14.280
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

